---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/winback';
const title = 'Winback campaigns: identity, eligibility and incremental contribution';
const desc = 'Plan a restrained winback journey around buying cycles, permission, exits, offer eligibility and incremental contribution.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Winback campaigns</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Invite a customer back when the buying context supports it</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A gap between orders is a question, not proof that a customer has left. Define what inactivity means for the product, confirm who bought it and whether you may contact them, then offer a relevant reason to return. A customer who returns naturally should not need a discount to do so.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Explore app evaluations</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">Plan campaign restraint</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Define the customer and the inactivity clock</h2>
      <p>Use a stable customer identifier linked to eligible completed orders. Record the event used to start the clock, its occurrence time, source and exclusions. A cancelled order should not silently restart the same clock as a fulfilled purchase. Decide how a fully returned order affects eligibility; a return may signal an unresolved problem rather than a reason to send another offer.</p>
      <p>Keep customer, household and order identity separate. A shared address or telephone number does not establish that two buyers are the same person or share permission. A gift recipient is not automatically the purchaser. If an authenticated account merge reveals a recent purchase, cancel the older winback entry. If identity remains uncertain, hold the entry instead of guessing. Document any household-level offer limit separately from channel permission.</p>
      <p>Estimate a buying window from comparable product cohorts and completed purchase histories, allowing for seasonality, gift cycles, durable goods and sparse data. There is no universal 90- or 180-day lapse threshold. A winter coat buyer may be waiting for the next season; a consumable buyer may belong in <a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">replenishment</a>. An active subscription, pause, skipped shipment or failed renewal needs its own service context. Cancellation should exit the generic journey and require a separately approved, permission-aware reactivation decision.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Permission and recency are separate gates</h2>
      <p>A past order establishes purchase history, not permission for a promotional text. Before entry and again before sending, check the intended channel, purpose, source evidence, withdrawal state and applicable sending restrictions. Recent site activity does not repair missing permission. Treat missing evidence, an uncertain number owner or stale subscription records as a hold requiring investigation, not as a sendable audience.</p>
      <p>Choose a review policy for older records based on how they were collected and what the customer expected. Do not use an unsolicited promotional SMS to ask whether SMS permission exists. Keep withdrawal history through imports and migrations; a profile recreation must not restore permission. For operational background, review <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet hours</a>; neither a segment label nor a tool setting establishes legal compliance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Give winback one place in the journey</h2>
      <p>Assign an owner to decide which journey may speak. A proposed priority is withdrawal and service resolution first, then active purchase or subscription context, then a product-specific request such as a restock alert, and only then generic winback. Adapt that order to the customer's request. Apply a shared contact budget across email, SMS and broadcasts; a winback flow must not bypass the budget because another platform sent the last message.</p>
      <p>Use a fresh eligibility check immediately before dispatch. A purchase exits the pending winback sequence across resolved identities; an open support case or return pauses it for resolution. A cancellation exits it for review. A withdrawal blocks the affected promotional channel or purpose, with broader suppression when requested. Never move someone to another channel merely to evade their expressed choice. Resolution of a support case does not automatically restart the old offer.</p>
      <p>A useful invitation can describe a relevant assortment change or ask for a preference through an already permitted channel. Use SMS only when the reason is concise, relevant and compatible with that customer's permission and contact budget. If the message needs substantial explanation, choose an eligible channel that supports it. Silence is a valid outcome when no useful reason exists.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Make the offer an enforceable promise</h2>
      <p>Write eligibility before choosing the discount: qualifying products, customer or household limits, excluded subscribers, minimum spend, stacking rules, stock, market, currency and redemption count. Version the offer and give its expiry an explicit timezone. Recheck both when the message is released; delaying a job must not extend an expired promise. Avoid artificial urgency or claims about limited stock that the store cannot substantiate.</p>
      <p>Confirm checkout enforcement, not only audience filtering. If an ineligible buyer receives a forwarded code, the redemption rules should still apply. If an offer becomes unavailable, stop pending messages and provide a clear support path for customers already contacted. Do not escalate incentives simply because someone ignored the first invitation. Set a stop condition and a new eligibility review before any later attempt.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Prevent stale jobs from becoming fresh mistakes</h2>
      <p>Keep a journey entry ID, customer ID, rule version and offer version alongside the scheduled job. Deduplicate source events by their durable event IDs and send attempts by an entry-and-step key. A duplicate order webhook must not create a second journey. A delayed purchase event should update history using its occurrence time and invalidate pending work even if it arrived after scheduling.</p>
      <p>Expire queued jobs after their useful context ends. On an outage, reconcile purchases, returns, support, cancellations, withdrawals and offer state before considering a replay. Do not release the backlog as a catch-up burst. If delivery acknowledgement is ambiguous, reconcile with the sender's message record before retrying; log unresolved cases for review instead of assuming a failed request means no message was sent. Retain the suppression ledger independently of retry state.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Hypothetical scenarios and proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>These are proposed acceptance fixtures for a store's implementation, not observed merchant outcomes or promises about any app.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Seasonal purchase:</strong> a coat buyer passes a generic inactivity cutoff before the next relevant season. Expect a seasonal review hold, with no generic discount dispatched solely because the cutoff passed.</li>
        <li><strong>Shared household:</strong> two buyers share a number and delivery address but have different customer records. Expect unresolved ownership to block SMS; neither buyer inherits the other's permission. Any household offer limit is evaluated separately.</li>
        <li><strong>Replenishment collision:</strong> a consumable buyer qualifies for replenishment and winback together. Expect the chosen journey owner to reserve one eligible contact slot and suppress the competing message.</li>
        <li><strong>Subscription change:</strong> a paused subscriber appears inactive and later cancels. Expect generic winback to remain blocked while paused and cancellation to exit its pending jobs for a distinct review.</li>
        <li><strong>Purchase before dispatch:</strong> a buyer orders through a merged account after entry. Expect the pending winback step to be cancelled after identity reconciliation, including when the order event arrives late.</li>
        <li><strong>Return, support and withdrawal:</strong> run each event separately against a scheduled invitation. Expect return/support to pause for resolution and withdrawal to block its affected promotional scope before dispatch. Clearing a ticket must not replay the old invitation.</li>
        <li><strong>Expired or forwarded offer:</strong> a delayed job references an expired version, and an excluded buyer tries a forwarded code. Expect the job to stop and checkout to reject the ineligible redemption under the stated rules.</li>
        <li><strong>Recovery and duplicate events:</strong> replay the same entry event twice, then restore service with an ambiguous send acknowledgement. Expect one entry, reconciliation before any retry, and no backlog burst or duplicate step.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Measure contribution and displaced demand</h2>
      <p>Randomly assign eligible customers to invitation and holdout groups before contact. Keep assignments stable through the observation window and later steps. If household offer sharing is material and identity is reliable, consider assignment at that level to reduce contamination. A holdout stays free of this winback treatment; record other campaigns received by both groups. Prevent operators from moving likely buyers out of the holdout after seeing their behavior.</p>
      <p>Use the same observation window and assigned-population denominator for each group. Include customers who never received or opened a message in the primary assignment-based comparison. Track repeat purchases, net revenue, returns, variable product and fulfilment costs, redeemed discounts, message charges and incremental support cost. Platform attribution alone cannot tell whether the invitation caused the order.</p>
      <p>For an illustrative calculation only, suppose two groups each contain 1,000 assigned customers. Contribution after product costs, discounts and returns is $3,600 for the invited group and $3,300 for holdout. The difference is $300. Subtract $80 in incremental delivery charges and $20 in extra support cost, neither already counted: $200 remains, or $0.20 per assigned customer. Allocate any additional setup and platform costs separately before deciding whether to expand. These invented values demonstrate accounting, not a forecast.</p>
      <p>Check whether discounted orders merely moved forward from the next buying window or displaced full-price purchases and other journeys. Observe long enough to include the expected cycle and returns; show uncertainty when the sample is small or seasonal. Report opt-outs and complaints beside contribution. Define pause thresholds in advance, and stop when evidence is insufficient to justify further contact.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Choose tools around the operating contract</h2>
      <p>A shared platform can fit when one team owns identity, exclusions and channel arbitration. Separate channel tools can fit when the team can synchronize those controls and reconcile delivery state. Ask each candidate to demonstrate your fixtures with your integrations before purchasing; this page makes no claim that a named supplier implements these controls automatically.</p>
      <p>Compare the <a class="link" href="/apps">app evaluations</a> using the same requirements: event freshness, custom entry rules, send-time exclusions, cancellation visibility, suppression export, retry reconciliation, offer enforcement and holdout reporting. Confirm current capabilities and costs directly with the supplier. Include the cost of operating any missing shared control.</p>
      <p>Continue with <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">email and SMS coordination</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a> and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics</a>. Reassess eligibility when buying cycles, identity quality or the offer changes, rather than automatically restarting a lapsed-customer sequence.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
